 of the military in sudan  over the past two years  certainly well you know as i said the  military are one uh part of the hybrid  transitional government that is uh  governing sudan's tradition uh in  advance of these elections scheduled for  2024. um  so much of the protesting against bashir  originally also you know included overt  uh calls for a move to a civilian  government and and of course by by by  extension a move away from civilians  from military dominance of sudan's  politics and and the economy and  certainly for the last 30 years of omar  bashir the military have dominated the  economy they they run the key sectors of  the economy and have always benefited  from from key key advantages there you  know in this transitional period they  they have unfortunately dragged their  feet and blocked key reforms  particularly on the economy  and other areas um and they've also sort  of offered this false dichotomy that  suggests that they're the only ones who  can  be the deliverers of security in sudan  when in fact you know what what the  protesters of the revolution sought was  for a a civilian command the control of  the military and for civilians to really  take charge of their own security rather  than having the military dominate  politically and in the security sector  jonas horner senior analyst for sudan  working for the international ngo crisis  group in khartoum thank you for uh for  talking to us today  thank you
 

